dating, provenance, and cultural-historical interpretation.",
    "raw_content_or_precise_summary": "The Heidelberg University Library page provides a digital facsimile of Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 monograph \"The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after‑world and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\" (London, 1925). The content is structured in two main parts:\n\n1. **I. Sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals 
from Thisbê, Boeotia (pp. 1–42)**\n   - Describes the discovery of a group of gold signet rings and bead‑seals in a tomb at Thisbê (ancient Thespiæ region, Boeotia, Greece). Evans discusses the findspot, 
circumstances of recovery, and the composition of the hoard.\n   - One of these rings is identified and named by Evans as the \"Ring of Nestor\". He presents detailed descriptions, drawings, and collotype 
plates of the ring and its bezel scene.\n   - Evans explains the ring’s **provenance** as coming from a sepulchral context at Thisbê in Boeotia, not from Pylos itself, but associates its imagery and style with 
Minoan Crete and the wider Aegean Bronze Age.\n   - In this section Evans reconstructs the discovery history: the tomb at Kakóvatos/Thísvi (keywords in metadata: \"Thísvi\", \"Kakóvatos\"), the deposit’s 
character as a treasure of gold signet rings and bead‑seals, and his own role in recognizing the significance and publishing the material.\n\n2. **II. A glimpse into the Minoan after‑world (pp. 43–75)**\n   - 
Offers Evans’s interpretive study of the iconography of the \"Ring of Nestor\" and associated rings.\n   - Evans situates the ring’s scenes in **Minoan religious and funerary ideology**, describing the 
depiction of a goddess, possible epiphany scenes, the underworld, and associated demons or spirits, arguing that the ring reflects beliefs about the afterlife.\n   - He proposes a **date in the Late Minoan 
period**, on stylistic and iconographic grounds, roughly corresponding to c. 1700–1450 BCE (Late Minoan I–II), and he compares the ring’s style to other Minoan gold signet rings.\n   - The monograph therefore 
provides an early scholarly **cultural period attribution** (Minoan, Late Bronze Age) and sets the object into the broader context of Minoan art and religion, and their interaction with mainland (Mycenaean) 
contexts.\n\n3. **Plates I–V**\n   - Include photographic and drawn plates of the rings and seals, showing the \"Ring of Nestor\" in detail, including its bezel engraving and band.\n   - These plates function 
as primary visual documentation of the ring’s condition and imagery in the early 20th century.\n\nThe digital facsimile itself does not transcribe the text but provides full-page images and download links (PDF 
49 MB and 175 MB) for the entire monograph. Bibliographic metadata list keywords \"Nestor; Ring; Minoische Kultur; Thísvi; Kakóvatos\" and identify the work’s DOI (10.11588/diglit.808). This source is the 
foundational description of the Ring of Nestor and its initial dating and interpretation."
  },
