      "document_summary": "Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 monograph presents the foundational description and interpretation of the object he names the \"Ring of Nestor\". In Part I he describes a sepulchral treasure 
of gold signet rings and bead‑seals discovered in a tomb at Thisbê in Boeotia, Greece, detailing the findspot, funerary context, composition of the hoard, and identifying one ring as the \"Ring of Nestor\". He 
emphasizes that the ring’s provenance is a tomb at Thisbê, not Pylos, though he links its style and imagery to Minoan Crete and the broader Aegean Bronze Age. He documents the ring with detailed descriptions, 
drawings, and collotype plates. In Part II he offers an extensive iconographic and religious interpretation of the ring and related pieces, situating their scenes in a Minoan religious and funerary ideology 
concerning the after‑world. He discusses depictions of a goddess, possible epiphany scenes, demonic or spirit figures, and underworld motifs, arguing that the ring reflects beliefs about the afterlife. Based on
stylistic and iconographic comparison to other Minoan gold signet rings, Evans proposes a Late Minoan date, roughly corresponding to the Late Bronze Age (around Late Minoan I–II), and uses the work to place the
object within Minoan art and religion and its interaction with mainland Mycenaean contexts. Plates I–V provide early 20th‑century visual documentation of the ring’s condition and imagery.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The Heidelberg University Library scroll‑view interface for Evans’s 1925 monograph provides navigational access and a mini table of contents confirming the structure of the work. It 
lists a first section on a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia (pages 1–42), a second section titled \"A glimpse into the Minoan after‑world\" (pages 43–75), and plates 
I–V. The interface confirms that the monograph contains detailed treatment of the discovery of a sepulchral treasure at Thisbê, including the Ring of Nestor, and an interpretive section placing the ring’s 
imagery in a Minoan afterlife context.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
