    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "The Metropolitan Museum of Art web essay \"Historic Images of the Greek Bronze Age\" discusses the history of visual documentation and reconstructions of Greek Bronze Age artifacts, 
focusing on the role of artists such as Gilliéron. Within this narrative the article mentions gold signet rings known as the Ring of Minos and the Ring of Nestor, alongside the Phaistos disk, as iconic Aegean 
Bronze Age objects whose images shaped scholarly and public perceptions of Minoan and Mycenaean culture. The text uses the Ring of Nestor as an example of a famous gold signet ring from the Aegean Bronze Age 
but does not provide technical, chronological, or discovery details.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "The ResearchGate entry for the scholarly work \"Demeter & Persephone at Mycenaean Pylos\" includes a figure caption referring to the \"Gold Ring of Nestor\". The caption identifies 
the ring as a gold ring from Pylos depicting a goddess, tentatively labeled U‑po‑jo‑po‑ti‑ni‑ja, in the underworld and assigns it a date range of approximately 1700–1450 BCE. The caption connects the ring’s 
imagery to underworld and goddess cults at Mycenaean Pylos, aligning its chronology with the Late Bronze Age. The available snippet does not describe the object’s findspot or discuss Evans’s Boeotian tomb 
context.",
      "source_ref": "s5"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d6",
      "document_summary": "Christina Hudler’s 2019 article \"No (e)scape? E‑scaping arguments in the interpretation of Bronze Age imagery\" examines methodological issues in interpreting imagery on Bronze Age 
seals and rings. She cites Arthur Evans’s study \"The Ring of Nestor\" as an influential example of elaborate narrative and epiphanic readings of Minoan and Mycenaean iconography. The article questions the 
extent to which such interpretations rely on speculative arguments and uses Evans’s claims about the Minoan after‑world to illustrate tendencies toward over‑interpretation. Hudler reinforces the status of the 
Ring of Nestor as a key case study in debates about the interpretation of Bronze Age religious imagery but does not readdress its discovery context or precise dating.",
      "source_ref": "s6"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d7",
      "document_summary": "The Academia.edu manuscript \"The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos\" discusses the assemblage of gold rings from the Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor
at Pylos and compares them to well‑known Aegean Bronze Age rings. In the available snippet, the authors mention the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos in relation to a comparison of band shapes and 
manufacture, noting that these rings have bands strikingly similar to a gold ring in Berlin without provenance. The discussion situates the Ring of Nestor among elite gold rings from the Aegean Bronze Age and 
uses it as a benchmark for stylistic and technical comparison in the context of Pylos and Crete, but the excerpt does not provide discovery details for the Ring of Nestor.",
      "source_ref": "s7"
    }
