 • The paper “SLAUGHTER, BLOOD AND SACRIFICE: MYCENAEAN *sphag- IN CONTEXT” uses Vlachopoulos’ discussion of the ‘Ring of Nestor’ as visual evidence for Mycenaean sacrificial imagery associated with *sphag-.   
   [9]                                                                                                                                                                                                            
 • A Late Bronze Age metal study cites A. J. Evans (1925), “‘The Ring of Nestor’: A glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a sepulchral ...,” when discussing the religious and funerary roles of metal          
   artefacts. [10]                                                                                                                                                                                                
 • The Dartmouth Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology Lesson 14 bibliography lists: “Evans, A. J. ‘The ‘Ring of Nestor’: A ...” among the recommended readings. [11]                                                    
 • A. G. Vlachopoulos authored a 2020 peer‑reviewed chapter titled "The Ring of Nestor and the Quest for Authenticity" in F. Blakolmer (ed.), Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography (Aegis
   18), pp. 223–252, which focuses on the authenticity, dating, imagery, and find context of the Ring of Nestor. [1][2][4]                                                                                        
 • Recent scholarship, especially the work of A. G. Vlachopoulos, argues that the Ring of Nestor is likely authentic rather than a modern forgery, synthesising technical analysis, stylistic comparison, and     
   iconographic evidence within the wider context of Aegean Bronze Age forgeries. [1][3]                                                                                                                          
 • Theodore Eliopoulos’s article "SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF the ‘Ring of Nestor’" in Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente analyses the ring’s imagery   
   and uses iconographic parallels, including incurved altars known from securely provenanced Aegean artefacts, to argue that iconography is a crucial tool for assessing the ring’s authenticity amid conflicting
   technical analyses. [3]                                                                                                                                                                                        
 • The Ring of Nestor is a large Aegean Bronze Age gold signet ring from Kakovatos (Messenia), assigned to the LM IB period (approximately 1530–1450 BC), with dimensions around 3.2–3.3 cm in length or c. 3.29 ×
   2.18 cm, and it is characterised in recent work as roughly the fifth-largest known Aegean gold signet ring. [1][5]                                                                                             
 • The Ring of Nestor is catalogued in the Corpus of Minoan and Mycenaean Seals as CMS VI no. 277 and is used in studies of Aegean Bronze Age glyptic, including analyses of handedness and body representation.  
   [7]                                                                                                                                                                                                            
 • The Ring of Nestor is repeatedly deployed in recent scholarship as visual evidence for Mycenaean or Aegean religious practice, including sacrificial imagery linked to the Mycenaean root *sphag-, bodily      
   posture and emotion, and the religious or funerary roles of Late Bronze Age metal artefacts. [6][9][10]                                                                                                        
 • Arthur J. Evans’s early 20th‑century paper "The 'Ring of Nestor': A glimpse into the Minoan after‑world and a sepulchral ..." interpreted the ring in terms of Minoan beliefs about the afterlife and emotion  
   (including a proposed primitive symbol for "surprise"), and this classic study continues to be cited both in modern research on Late Bronze Age metals and in contemporary teaching on Aegean prehistory, while
   its symbolic interpretations are being reassessed. [6][10][11]                                                                                                                                                 
 • The Ring of Nestor is used as a case study in social‑theoretical work on Aegean Bronze Age golden signet rings, including the article "The 'Knossos Effect': Golden Signets as Visual Markers of Social        
   Dependencies in the Aegean Bronze Age", which treats the ring as an example of how gold signets may have visualised social dependency and possible ties to Knossian networks. [8]                              

