 • Arthur J. Evans authored the monograph titled "The ring of Nestor: a glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia," published in    
   London in 1925 and held in major library catalogues. [22][24]                                                                                                                                                  
 • Evans’s monograph and associated records describe the "Ring of Nestor" as part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals excavated from a tomb at Thisbê in Boeotia, Greece. [20][24]       
 • The design on the "Ring of Nestor" bezel, as described by Evans, includes multiple human figures, architectural or columnar structures, trees, and religious symbols, and is illustrated with plates and       
   figures in his 1925 monograph. [20]                                                                                                                                                                            
 • Evans interpreted the scene on the "Ring of Nestor" as representing a "Minoan after-world" and connected it to Minoan–Mycenaean religious beliefs and funerary practices in the Aegean Bronze Age. [20][21]    
 • Evans explicitly linked the "Ring of Nestor" to the Homeric figure Nestor and used this literary association to justify the ring’s modern name. [20]                                                           
 • Evans’s 1925 study of the "Ring of Nestor" and the Thisbê gold signet-rings and bead-seals was first published as an article in the Journal of Hellenic Studies and is now available in full-text PDF via      
   platforms such as JSTOR and indexed by Cambridge and SciSpace. [21][25][26]                                                                                                                                    
 • Library and catalog records (HathiTrust and the National Library of Ireland) classify Evans’s "The ring of Nestor" under subjects such as Minoan civilization, Mycenaean art, ancient signet rings, and        
   Boeotian antiquities, indicating its focus on Aegean prehistoric archaeology and art history. [22][24]                                                                                                         
 • Evans interpreted the Thisbê hoard of gold signet-rings and bead-seals, including the "Ring of Nestor," as a group with religious and funerary significance. [21][23]                                          
 • Nature published a contemporary review of Evans’s work "The ring of Nestor: a glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia,"        
   summarizing his discussion of the Thisbê gold rings and their relation to a Minoan afterworld and offering evaluative comments on its scholarly value. [23]                                                    
 • Modern scholarship, including the article "Some observations on the iconography of the 'Ring of Nestor'," re-examines the iconography of the "Ring of Nestor" in the broader context of Aegean forgeries and   
   authenticity debates, reassessing Evans’s interpretation and considering whether the ring might be a modern forgery or altered object. [3]                                                                     

Establish the discovery, dating, provenance, and historical-cultural context of the Ring of Nestor, including where, when, and by whom it was found and how it has been dated and interpreted in relation to      
Mycenaean or other relevant periods.                                                                                                                                                                              

 • Evans presents the Ring of Nestor as part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from a tomb at Thisbê in Boeotia, describing the findspot and funerary context of the hoard. [27]       
 • Evans explicitly notes that the Ring of Nestor was found at Thisbê in Boeotia and not at Pylos, while linking its style and imagery to Minoan Crete and the Aegean Bronze Age. [27]                            
 • In Part II of his monograph, Evans argues that the Ring of Nestor’s iconography belongs to Minoan religious and funerary ideology, depicting a goddess, possible epiphany scenes, demons or spirits, and       
   elements of the underworld that together suggest beliefs about the afterlife. [27]                                                                                                                             
 • Evans dates the Ring of Nestor to the Late Minoan period on stylistic and iconographic grounds, roughly corresponding to the Late Bronze Age and comparable to other Minoan gold signet rings. [27]            
 • The Heidelberg scroll‑view interface lists the sections of Evans’s monograph as: "1–42 I. Sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia", "43–75 II. A glimpse into the Minoan  
   after‑world", and "Tafel I–V". [28]                                                                                                                                                                            
 • The Scribd caption reads: "FIGURE 11 Signet ring (the ‘Ring of Nestor’). Minoan, about 1500 BCE. Gold, H. 2.2 × L. 3.9 cm (7⁄8 × 19⁄16 in.). Oxford, Ashmolean Museum, AN1938.1130." [29]                      
 • The Metropolitan Museum essay refers to "gold signet rings known as the Ring of Minos and the Ring of Nestor, and the well-known disk from Phaistos on Crete" as examples of artifacts whose historic images   
   shaped understanding of the Greek Bronze Age. [17]                                                                                                                                                             
 • A figure caption in the work "Demeter & Persephone at Mycenaean Pylos" labels "Figure 14: Gold Ring of Nestor – Goddess U‑po‑jo‑po‑ti‑ni‑ja (?) in the Underworld, Pylos (ca. 1700–1450 BCE)." [30]            
 • Hudler’s article explicitly cites Arthur Evans’s "‘The Ring of Nestor’: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and a Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia" as an example
   in discussions of interpretative approaches to Bronze Age imagery. [19]                                                                                                                                        
 • Hudler characterizes Evans’s interpretations of the Ring of Nestor’s imagery and the Minoan after‑world as illustrative of elaborate narrative readings that may rest on speculative arguments. [19]           
 • In a snippet from "The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos", the authors state that "Both the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos have bands strikingly similar ... 30, a gold ring in Berlin
   without provenance. See Furtwängler 1900, pp ...", indicating a comparison of band shapes and manufacture. [31]                                                                                                
 • According to Arthur Evans’s 1925 monograph, the Ring of Nestor was discovered as part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead‑seals in a tomb at Thisbê in Boeotia, Greece, and he explicitly   
   states that its findspot is Thisbê rather than Pylos. [27][28]                                                                                                                                                 
 • Modern secondary sources, including a figure caption in "Demeter & Persephone at Mycenaean Pylos", treat the Gold Ring of Nestor as a ring from Pylos and connect it specifically with Mycenaean Pylos and its 
   elite or cultic context. [29][30][31]                                                                                                                                                                          
 • The Ring of Nestor is a gold signet ring of the Aegean Bronze Age, widely recognized in museum and scholarly literature as a famous or iconic artifact, and it is catalogued in the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford 
   under inventory number AN1938.1130, with dimensions approximately 2.2 cm in height and 3.9 cm in length. [17][27][29]                                                                                          
 • Arthur Evans dates the Ring of Nestor to the Late Minoan period, roughly corresponding to the Late Bronze Age (approximately Late Minoan I–II), and modern secondary sources commonly place it around 1500 BCE 
   or within a broader ca. 1700–1450 BCE range. [27][29][30]                                                                                                                                                      
 • Evans and subsequent commentators attribute the Ring of Nestor stylistically and culturally to Minoan Crete and the wider Aegean Bronze Age, even though its reported findspot is on the Greek mainland at     
   Thisbê in Boeotia. [27][29]                                                                                                                                                                                    
 • Evans interprets the Ring of Nestor’s scenes as expressing Minoan religious and funerary ideology concerning the afterlife, including a goddess, possible epiphany scenes, demons or spirits, and underworld   
   motifs; later scholars such as Hudler cite this work but criticize Evans’s elaborate narrative reading as potentially speculative. [19][27][30]                                                                
 • Scholarship on Aegean Bronze Age gold rings uses the Ring of Nestor, together with the Ring of Minos and other unprovenanced gold rings, for stylistic and technical comparison of band shapes, manufacture,   
   and imagery, particularly in studies of elite burials such as the Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos. [17][31]                                                                                                      

